20:T584,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BlogPosting","@id":"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/games-teach-curiosity-without-lecture/#article","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/games-teach-curiosity-without-lecture"},"headline":"When a game teaches curiosity without a lecture","description":"How interactive systems can invite experimentation, and what MIT’s maker-learning work suggests about putting agency before explanation.","image":["https://www.outbrick.site/blog/games-teach-curiosity-without-lecture.png"],"datePublished":"2026-09-04","dateModified":"2026-09-04","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Mourad Hamdi","description":"Mourad makes small, thoughtful games for the space between one task and the next. OutBrick is his ongoing experiment in calmer play."},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"OutBrick","url":"https://www.outbrick.site","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.outbrick.site/icon.png"}},"articleSection":"Learning through play","keywords":"learning games, curiosity, MIT, game design","citation":["https://news.mit.edu/2022/3-questions-dishita-turakhia-teaching-computational-maker-skills-through-gaming-0714","https://doi.org/10.1037/a0019440","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6453078/","https://doi.org/10.1037/a0034857"]}15:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$20"}}]
16:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"OutBrick\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Journal\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site/blog\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"When a game teaches curiosity without a lecture\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/games-teach-curiosity-without-lecture\"}]}"}}]
17:["$","h2",null,{"children":"Curiosity starts with a question"}]
18:["$","p","A game teaches when the player wants to know what will happen next. That desire can begin with a visible mismatch, a responsive object, or a rule that is simple enough to test. The player is not waiting for the tutorial to finish; they are already constructing a model.",{"children":"A game teaches when the player wants to know what will happen next. That desire can begin with a visible mismatch, a responsive object, or a rule that is simple enough to test. The player is not waiting for the tutorial to finish; they are already constructing a model."}]
19:["$","p","The model can be wrong. That is part of the value. A safe mistake gives the player a new piece of evidence and keeps authorship on their side of the screen.",{"children":"The model can be wrong. That is part of the value. A safe mistake gives the player a new piece of evidence and keeps authorship on their side of the screen."}]
1a:["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","mit-fabo-2022",{"href":"#reference-mit-fabo-2022","aria-label":"Jump to source: Turakhia (2022)","children":["[",1,"]"]}],["$","a","motivation-2010",{"href":"#reference-motivation-2010","aria-label":"Jump to source: Przybylski, Rigby, & Ryan (2010)","children":["[",2,"]"]}]]]}]
1b:["$","section","making-is-a-form-of-thinking",{"id":"making-is-a-form-of-thinking","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Making is a form of thinking"}],[["$","p","MIT News describes work that teaches computational maker skills through gaming, connecting game-like activities with creating and tinkering. The article is a university news account, not a randomized evaluation of every game-based learning outcome, so the useful claim is about design posture: let learners make something, see it respond, and share the result.",{"children":"MIT News describes work that teaches computational maker skills through gaming, connecting game-like activities with creating and tinkering. The article is a university news account, not a randomized evaluation of every game-based learning outcome, so the useful claim is about design posture: let learners make something, see it respond, and share the result."}],["$","p","That posture turns explanation into a consequence of activity. A player understands a system more deeply when the explanation answers a question they have already felt in their hands.",{"children":"That posture turns explanation into a consequence of activity. A player understands a system more deeply when the explanation answers a question they have already felt in their hands."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","mit-fabo-2022",{"href":"#reference-mit-fabo-2022","aria-label":"Jump to source: Turakhia (2022)","children":["[",1,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
1c:["$","section","the-outbrick-question",{"id":"the-outbrick-question","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"The OutBrick question"}],[["$","p","OutBrick’s boards are small enough to ask a focused question: if this brick moves first, which lane stays open? The player can try, undo, compare, and eventually recognize the relationship. The game does not need to call this a lesson for the player to practice reasoning.",{"children":"OutBrick’s boards are small enough to ask a focused question: if this brick moves first, which lane stays open? The player can try, undo, compare, and eventually recognize the relationship. The game does not need to call this a lesson for the player to practice reasoning."}],["$","p","The important part is that the feedback is truthful. A move should change the board in a way the player can inspect. Mystery can create curiosity at the start, but clarity is what lets curiosity become understanding.",{"children":"The important part is that the feedback is truthful. A move should change the board in a way the player can inspect. Mystery can create curiosity at the start, but clarity is what lets curiosity become understanding."}]],["$","ul",null,{"className":"article-bullets","children":[["$","li","Show the question in the layout",{"children":"Show the question in the layout"}],["$","li","Let experiments be reversible",{"children":"Let experiments be reversible"}],["$","li","Give feedback that explains the state",{"children":"Give feedback that explains the state"}],["$","li","Reward a better model, not a lucky guess",{"children":"Reward a better model, not a lucky guess"}]]}],null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","mit-fabo-2022",{"href":"#reference-mit-fabo-2022","aria-label":"Jump to source: Turakhia (2022)","children":["[",1,"]"]}],["$","a","flow-review-2019",{"href":"#reference-flow-review-2019","aria-label":"Jump to source: Ma et al. (2019)","children":["[",3,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
1d:["$","section","leave-with-a-better-question",{"id":"leave-with-a-better-question","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Leave with a better question"}],[["$","p","A good learning game does not end every session with a score that claims to summarize the player. It leaves them with a sharper question for the next board. That is a quieter measure of progress, but it is often the one that belongs to the player.",{"children":"A good learning game does not end every session with a score that claims to summarize the player. It leaves them with a sharper question for the next board. That is a quieter measure of progress, but it is often the one that belongs to the player."}],["$","p","The OutBrick version of teaching is modest: help someone notice one relationship, make one deliberate choice, and feel that their attention changed what became possible.",{"children":"The OutBrick version of teaching is modest: help someone notice one relationship, make one deliberate choice, and feel that their attention changed what became possible."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","mit-fabo-2022",{"href":"#reference-mit-fabo-2022","aria-label":"Jump to source: Turakhia (2022)","children":["[",1,"]"]}],["$","a","benefits-2014",{"href":"#reference-benefits-2014","aria-label":"Jump to source: Granic, Lobel, & Engels (2014)","children":["[",4,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
